\chapter{Pipes}
\label{ch:pipes}

A pipe connects two processes: what the one writes, the other reads. Nothing else in Unix is as small and as useful. The shell
uses it to join programs (\texttt{ps | grep ssh}), and a daemon uses it to talk to the program it has started (the SSH server
later feeds a shell through two pipes). The book mentions pipes only to say that they are not there: the kernel could ``create
an internal buffer for each pipe and associate two negative file descriptors with it''. This chapter does that, in the way the
other kinds of files already work.

\section{What a pipe is}

A pipe is a ring buffer in the kernel with two ends. The \emph{write end} puts bytes in, the \emph{read end} takes them out, in
the same order. Four rules make it behave like a pipe:

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{read} on an empty pipe \emph{sleeps} until somebody writes, but if no process has the write end open any more
  (every writer closed it or ended), it returns 0: the end of the file.
\item \texttt{write} on a full pipe sleeps until somebody has read, so a fast writer cannot overtake a slow reader.
\item \texttt{write} on a pipe without a reader is an error: the writer gets the signal \texttt{SIGPIPE}, which ends it unless
  it ignores the signal (then \texttt{write} returns $-1$). Nobody would ever read the bytes.
\item A pipe lives as long as one of its ends is open, in any process. \texttt{fork} gives the child the same two ends, and
  every process that ends closes its files, so the counts are right without further work.
\end{itemize}

\section{Where it fits: one more kind of file}

A file descriptor of a process (\texttt{files[pfd]} in the process table) holds a \emph{global} descriptor, and the book builds
that number as \texttt{(file system * 256) + number inside the file system}: the Minix file system is 1, the devices are 3. A
pipe becomes file system number 4 (\texttt{FS\_PIPE}), and the number inside it says which pipe and which end:
\texttt{pipe * 2 + end} (0 = read end, 1 = write end). Nothing else needs to know about it.

The book's \texttt{u\_read}, \texttt{u\_write}, \texttt{u\_close} and \texttt{u\_reopen} look at that number and call the
right file system. They are compiled unchanged (the book stays the single source, see \texttt{PRINCIPLES.md}), but the build
renames them to \texttt{book\_u\_read} and so on, as it does for the scheduler and \texttt{u\_kill}. The functions of this chapter
have the book's names, look whether the descriptor is a pipe, and pass everything else on to the book:

<<pipes.c: wrappers>>=
int u_read (int fd, void *buf, int nbyte)  { return is_pipe (fd) ? pipe_read (fd, buf, nbyte)  : book_u_read (fd, buf, nbyte); }
int u_write (int fd, void *buf, int nbyte) { return is_pipe (fd) ? pipe_write (fd, buf, nbyte) : book_u_write (fd, buf, nbyte); }
int u_close (int fd)                       { if (!is_pipe (fd)) return book_u_close (fd);  pipe_close (fd);  return 0; }

// fork () copies a file descriptor with u_reopen: for a file that is a new position in the file, for a pipe end it is one more
// user of the same end
int u_reopen (int fd) {
  if (!is_pipe (fd)) return book_u_reopen (fd);
  struct pipe *p = &pipes[(fd & 0xff) / 2];
  if (fd & 1) p->writers++;  else p->readers++;
  return fd;
}
@

That is the whole connection to the rest of the kernel. \texttt{syscall\_read}, \texttt{syscall\_write}, \texttt{syscall\_close}, \texttt{fork}
and \texttt{exit} reach the pipe through these four functions and need no change.

\section{The data structures}

There are 16 pipes of 4~KByte. A fixed table is enough for a teaching system, and a full table is an ordinary error
(\texttt{pipe} returns $-1$) instead of a case that needs memory management. The ring buffer has a read position and a count (the
write position is the sum of the two, modulo the size). \texttt{readers} and \texttt{writers} count the \emph{descriptors} that
refer to an end, in all processes.

All processes that wait for any pipe sleep in one queue, \texttt{pipe\_queue}. Whenever something happens on a pipe (data
arrived, space is free, an end was closed) \emph{all} sleepers wake and look again whether their pipe is ready; a wrong guess
costs one look and one more sleep. One queue instead of one per pipe keeps the code at a single line per wake-up and cannot lose
a wake-up; for 16 pipes that is fast enough.

<<pipes.c: pipe table>>=
#define FS_PIPE      4                        // the file system number of pipes (Minix is 1, devices are 3)
#define MAX_PIPES    16
#define PIPE_SIZE    4096
#define TSTATE_WAITPIPE 13                    // a process sleeps in pipe_queue (12 is the serial disk)

struct pipe {
  int  used;
  int  readers, writers;                      // descriptors that refer to the read end / the write end
  int  start, count;                          // the bytes are buf[start ...], count of them (modulo PIPE_SIZE)
  char buf[PIPE_SIZE];
};

static struct pipe pipes[MAX_PIPES];
static blocked_queue pipe_queue;              // everybody who waits for any pipe

static int is_pipe (int gfd) { return gfd >= 0 && (gfd >> 8) == FS_PIPE; }

static void pipe_wake_all (void) {            // something changed: the sleepers look again
  while (pipe_queue.next != 0) deblock (pipe_queue.next, &pipe_queue);
}

// a process may only sleep if it is a real process: the idle thread and the boot code poll (see the book)
static int can_sleep (void) { return current_task > 1 && scheduler_is_active; }
@

\section{Reading, writing, closing}

\texttt{pipe\_read} sleeps while the pipe is empty and a writer exists. It takes what is there (at most what was asked for) and
returns it at once; it does \emph{not} wait for the buffer to fill, which is what a reader on a terminal expects, too. A read
from the write end is an error.

<<pipes.c: read>>=
static int pipe_read (int gfd, void *buf, int n) {
  struct pipe *p = &pipes[(gfd & 0xff) / 2];
  if (gfd & 1) return -1;                                   // that is the write end
  while (p->count == 0) {
    if (p->writers == 0) return 0;                          // nobody can write any more: end of file
    if (!can_sleep ()) return -1;
    block (&pipe_queue, TSTATE_WAITPIPE);
    arch_resign ();                                         // here the process sleeps
  }
  if (n > p->count) n = p->count;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    ((char *)buf)[i] = p->buf[(p->start + i) % PIPE_SIZE];
  p->start = (p->start + n) % PIPE_SIZE;
  p->count -= n;
  pipe_wake_all ();                                         // there is room now
  return n;
}
@

\texttt{pipe\_write} puts all bytes in, in as many rounds as it takes: whenever the buffer is full it sleeps until a reader has made room. If
the last reader is gone, the writer gets \texttt{SIGPIPE} (the book's \texttt{u\_kill} turns it into a kill for a process that does not
handle it, and then the call below does not return) and, if it survives, the error $-1$ --- or the number of bytes that were
written before the readers left.

<<pipes.c: write>>=
static int pipe_write (int gfd, void *buf, int n) {
  struct pipe *p = &pipes[(gfd & 0xff) / 2];
  if (!(gfd & 1)) return -1;                                // that is the read end
  int done = 0;
  while (done < n) {
    if (p->readers == 0) {                                  // nobody will ever read this
      u_kill (current_task, SIGPIPE);
      return done > 0 ? done : -1;
    }
    if (p->count == PIPE_SIZE) {
      if (!can_sleep ()) return done > 0 ? done : -1;
      block (&pipe_queue, TSTATE_WAITPIPE);
      arch_resign ();
      continue;                                             // look again
    }
    int room = PIPE_SIZE - p->count;
    int chunk = n - done < room ? n - done : room;
    for (int i = 0; i < chunk; i++)
      p->buf[(p->start + p->count + i) % PIPE_SIZE] = ((char *)buf)[done + i];
    p->count += chunk;
    done += chunk;
    pipe_wake_all ();                                       // there is something to read
  }
  return done;
}
@

Closing an end only counts down. The last close of both ends frees the table entry. In any case the sleepers wake up, because a
reader may now see the end of the file and a writer the missing reader.

<<pipes.c: close>>=
static void pipe_close (int gfd) {
  struct pipe *p = &pipes[(gfd & 0xff) / 2];
  if (gfd & 1) { if (p->writers > 0) p->writers--; }
  else         { if (p->readers > 0) p->readers--; }
  if (p->readers == 0 && p->writers == 0) p->used = 0;      // nothing refers to it: the entry is free
  pipe_wake_all ();
}
@

\section{When a process is killed}

A process that ends with \texttt{exit} closes its files in the book's \texttt{syscall\_exit}, and so its pipe ends are closed by the
function above. A process that is \emph{killed} (a signal without a handler, a crash, \texttt{kill -9}) does not run
\texttt{syscall\_exit}: its table entry is simply marked free. Two things must be done for a pipe, and the function
\texttt{pipes\_before\_kill} does them. The kernel calls it in \texttt{u\_kill} (chapter on the processors, \texttt{smp.c}) just before it
passes the signal on to the book:

\begin{enumerate}
\item A process that sleeps in a pipe must leave the queue before it can be killed: the book's \texttt{u\_kill} knows only its own
  queues. \texttt{deblock} makes it ready, and the book's function then removes it from the ready queue as for any other process.
\item If the signal ends the process (\texttt{SIGKILL}, or a signal without handler whose default is to end it, as in the book's
  list), its pipe ends are closed now. Otherwise the reader at the other end would wait for ever for an end of file.
\end{enumerate}

<<pipes.c: kill>>=
// does the book's u_kill end the process? (the list of the book: default action of these signals is to terminate)
static int signal_ends_process (TCB *t, int signo) {
  if (signo == SIGKILL) return 1;
  if (t->sighandlers[signo] != SIG_DFL) return 0;
  return signo == SIGABRT || signo == SIGALRM || signo == SIGBUS || signo == SIGFPE || signo == SIGHUP || signo == SIGILL ||
         signo == SIGINT  || signo == SIGPIPE || signo == SIGTERM || signo == SIGUSR1 || signo == SIGUSR2 || signo == SIGPROF ||
         signo == SIGSYS  || signo == SIGTRAP || signo == SIGVTALRM || signo == SIGXCPU || signo == SIGXFSZ;
}

void pipes_before_kill (int pid, int signo) {
  if (pid < 3 || pid >= MAX_THREADS || signo < 0 || signo > 31) return;
  TCB *t = &thread_table[pid];
  if (!t->used) return;
  if (t->state == TSTATE_WAITPIPE) deblock (pid, &pipe_queue);          // ready again; the book removes it from the ready queue
  if (signal_ends_process (t, signo))
    for (int pfd = 0; pfd < MAX_PFD; pfd++)
      if (is_pipe (t->files[pfd])) { pipe_close (t->files[pfd]);  t->files[pfd] = -1; }
}
@

\section{The system calls \texttt{pipe} and \texttt{dup2}}

\texttt{pipe (fds)} makes a new pipe and returns the read end in \texttt{fds[0]} and the write end in \texttt{fds[1]}, both as
file descriptors of the calling process (the lowest free ones). It can fail for two reasons that both give $-1$: the table of pipes
is full, or the process has no two free descriptors.

The two ends are not put in with \texttt{gfd2pfd}: that function first looks whether the global number is already in the table,
and there it \emph{is} legitimate for the same pipe end to appear twice (after \texttt{dup2}), so the system call looks for free
entries itself.

<<pipes.c: pipe and dup2>>=
static int free_pfd (TCB *t, int from) {
  for (int pfd = from; pfd < MAX_PFD; pfd++) if (t->files[pfd] == -1) return pfd;
  return -1;
}

void syscall_pipe (context_t *r) {
  int *fds = (int *) r->ebx;
  TCB *t = &thread_table[thread_table[current_task].pid];
  int n = 0, rfd = free_pfd (t, 0), wfd = rfd < 0 ? -1 : free_pfd (t, rfd + 1);
  while (n < MAX_PIPES && pipes[n].used) n++;
  if (n == MAX_PIPES || rfd < 0 || wfd < 0) { eax_return (-1); }
  pipes[n].used = 1;  pipes[n].readers = pipes[n].writers = 1;  pipes[n].start = pipes[n].count = 0;
  t->files[rfd] = (FS_PIPE @<< 8) | (n * 2);                // the read end
  t->files[wfd] = (FS_PIPE @<< 8) | (n * 2 + 1);            // the write end
  fds[0] = rfd;
  fds[1] = wfd;
  eax_return (0);
}
@

\texttt{dup2 (old, new)} makes descriptor \texttt{new} another name for what \texttt{old} refers to; if \texttt{new} was open, it is
closed first. This is how a program that starts another one decides what the new program finds as its standard input and output:
the child calls \texttt{dup2 (fd, 1)} and then \texttt{exec}, and everything that the new program prints goes into the file or
the pipe. (A file that is copied this way gets its own read/write position through \texttt{u\_reopen}, the same as after a
\texttt{fork}. The standard input, output and error are the negative numbers \texttt{DEV\_STDIN} and so on, which need no
reference count.)

<<pipes.c: pipe and dup2>>=
void syscall_dup2 (context_t *r) {
  int old = r->ebx, new = r->ecx;
  TCB *t = &thread_table[thread_table[current_task].pid];
  if (old < 0 || old >= MAX_PFD || new < 0 || new >= MAX_PFD || t->files[old] == -1) { eax_return (-1); }
  if (old == new) { eax_return (new); }
  int gfd = t->files[old];
  if (t->files[new] != -1) u_close (t->files[new]);
  t->files[new] = gfd >= 0 ? u_reopen (gfd) : gfd;
  eax_return (new);
}
@

